.TH GPUBENCH 1
.SH NAME
gpubench \- time a fem(2) solve on the cpu against the GPU backend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpubench
[
.I n
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Gpubench
assembles and solves the same
.IR fem (2)
Poisson problem twice, once with
.B "backend cpu"
and once with
.BR "backend gpu precision f32" ,
and prints assembly time, solve time, iteration count, residual and a
sample of the solution for each.
.I N
gives the mesh size and defaults to 12, meaning 12x12x12.
.PP
It exists because whether offloading helps depends entirely on what it
is compared against, and the answer is not the one people assume.
.PP
It used to report a break-even around nine thousand nodes, above which
the GPU won.
That was measured against a matvec written in Limbo.
Since
.IR sparse (2)
began calling
.IR math (2)'s
.B spmv
builtin, which is nine to ten times faster, there is no break-even at
any size that fits in memory here:
.IP
.EX
mesh        nodes   cpu f64   gpu f32
12x12x12     2197      0ms      74ms
16x16x16     4913      2ms      36ms
20x20x20     9261      2ms      34ms
24x24x24    15625      6ms      30ms
.EE
.PP
The GPU column is roughly flat while the CPU column grows with the
problem, which says that path is dominated by per-call cost rather than
arithmetic.
Single precision also costs extra CG iterations \(em 39 against 34 at
24\(mu24\(mu24 \(em so the two columns are not even the same solve, and
any speedup quoted from them should carry both iteration counts.
.PP
The lesson worth keeping is about the baseline, not the GPU: an
offload measured against an interpreted loop is measuring the
interpreter.
.PP
Large meshes need more heap than the default:
.EX
	emu -pheap=1073741824 -pmain=536870912 -r . /dis/gpubench.dis 30
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gpubench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpu (2),
.IR gpu (3),
.IR fem (2)
.SH BUGS
It reports wall-clock time for one run of each, with no repetition or
warm-up, so small differences are noise.
It compares f64 on the cpu against f32 on the GPU, which is the only
comparison the hardware allows but is not like for like.
